$ yo explain E0614
E0614 — a trait method cannot be called through a Dyn

A `Dyn(Trait)` value erases its concrete type and calls its methods through a vtable, one function per method. A method whose signature mentions `Self` anywhere but the receiver (a `Self` parameter, a `Self` or `Option(Self)` result) has no single C type at the call once the concrete type is erased, and a method with `generic(...)` parameters has no single function to put in the vtable. Such methods get no vtable slot, so calling one through a `Dyn` is an error. Forming the `Dyn` and calling its other methods is fine. A method that takes `self` by value has no slot either, and forming a `Dyn` over its trait is rejected for every payload (plans/VALUES_BY_DEFAULT.md decision 49): a `Dyn` is unsized and lives behind the pointer that owns it, so there is no value to hand the method, and a call would move the payload out of that pointer.

`Future` cannot be listed in a `Dyn`, in any position: a parameter, a field, a list element, or a generic alias over a `Trait` binder. A future is one pointer to its state machine, and awaiting or spawning it consumes it. One future is typed `Impl(Future(T, E))`. Several different running tasks are kept as `JoinHandle(T)` (`io.spawn(f, io)`; `join_all` and `race` in `std/async` take an `ArrayList(JoinHandle(T))`).

Call the method on a concrete value instead, or declare it so that `Self` appears only as the receiver and it takes no `generic(...)` parameters. Before this was an error the call passed `check` and failed inside the C compiler.

Example — this fails:
  Sp :: trait(speak : (fn(self : &Self) -> i32), me : (fn(self : &Self) -> Self));
  Cat :: struct(n : i32);
  derive(Cat, Copy, Clone);
  impl(Cat, Sp(speak : (fn(self : &Self) -> i32)(self.n), me : (fn(self : &Self) -> Self)(self.*)));
  main :: (fn() -> unit)({
    (d : Box(Dyn(Sp))) = dyn(Box.new(Cat(n : i32(3))));
    c := d.me();
  });
  
Example — write this instead:
  Sp :: trait(speak : (fn(self : &Self) -> i32), me : (fn(self : &Self) -> Self));
  Cat :: struct(n : i32);
  derive(Cat, Copy, Clone);
  impl(Cat, Sp(speak : (fn(self : &Self) -> i32)(self.n), me : (fn(self : &Self) -> Self)(self.*)));
  main :: (fn() -> unit)({
    (d : Box(Dyn(Sp))) = dyn(Box.new(Cat(n : i32(3))));
    n := d.speak();
  });
  
  `me` returns `Self`, whose concrete type the `Dyn(Sp)` erased; `speak` returns `i32` and is callable.
Run 'yo explain --list' for all codes.
